#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af9aa6af-5cbe-5933-9c7b-62e32a63386c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Prime Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "580e631f-10aa-5f3c-92a9-592c879df478" :question/math-academy-id "e-10623" :question/problem "Which of the following is a list containing only prime numbers?\n\n1. $3, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 17$\n2. $3, \\: 7, \\: 11, \\: 29$\n3. $2, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 13$" :question/worked-solution "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in each list.\n\n- List I includes $8$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1$, $2$, $4$, and $8$.\n- List II contains only prime numbers since $3$, $7$, $11$, and $29$ have only two factors each: $1$ and themselves.\n- List III contains $4$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1$, $2$, and $4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd997467-85ee-5288-a574-2719cbff167b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Composite Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "f1e2d3d3-8819-5988-8ec1-195cbd548773" :question/math-academy-id "e-10624" :question/problem "Which of the following lists contains only composite numbers?\n\n1. $4, \\: 16, \\: 24, \\: 28$\n2. $6, \\: 15, \\: 19, \\: 20$\n3. $8, \\: 15, \\: 21, \\: 29$" :question/worked-solution "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$. A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each list.\n\n- List I contains only composite numbers. Notice that all of them have $2$ as a factor.\n- List II includes $19$, which is prime.\n- List III contains $29$, which is prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b45eb1fc-7e00-5ece-8dcf-f27e6d4fb78e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying One as Neither Prime Nor Composite"}] :question/id #uuid "72d7834c-fdfd-5f05-ad5f-cd9f6a2fcffc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10625" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are prime?\n$13, \\quad 15, \\quad 17, \\quad 1, \\quad 6$" :question/worked-solution "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$. A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13$.\n- $15$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1$. For example, $3$ is a factor of $15$:\n$15= 3\\times 5$\n- $17$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $17$.\n- $1$ is *not* prime since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* composite either.\n- $6$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1$. For example, $3$ is a factor of $6$:\n$6= 3\\times 2$\n\nTherefore, only $13$ and $17$ are prime."}]}
